<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herborn Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1584">1584</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hohe Schule Herborn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a> Academy (Hohe Schule Herborn) was a Calvinist college of university rank founded in 1584 by Count John VI of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> at Herborn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Teachers such as Caspar Olevian, Johannes Piscator and Johannes Althusius made it a centre of Reformed theology and political thought, and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-amos-comenius">John Amos Comenius</a> studied there. It declined in the 18th century and closed in 1817, continuing as a seminary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herborn-academy.jpg" alt="Herborn Academy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver Abels (SBT) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herborn_-_Schloss_R%C3%BCckseite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Count John VI of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> founded the school, the Academia Nassauensis, in 1584 at the request of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, Prince of Orange, in the year of William's death. The count granted the students two warm meals and three litres of small beer a day. The academy was first housed in Herborn Castle, and in 1588 John bought and enlarged the old town hall for its use. It later took on a distinctly Calvinist character and grew to four faculties, much like a conventional university.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a> soon became one of the most important places of learning of the Reformed movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, known as a centre of encyclopaedic Ramism and the birthplace of covenant theology and pansophism. It held that every theory must prove itself in practical use and so be morally instructive. Johannes Piscator published a Reformed translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> there between 1602 and 1604.</p><p>Despite repeated efforts and the quality of its teaching, it never received imperial permission to call itself a university, largely because it was a Calvinist foundation, and so it could never award doctorates. The academy moved to the lower castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> from 1594 to 1599 or 1600 and again from 1606 to 1609.</p><p>Its first heyday lasted until 1626, with more than 300 students, about 400 in 1603. Numbers fell sharply afterwards, rose to a second peak from 1685 to 1725 and then averaged only about 100; at one point in 1745 fewer than five students were in the town. In all about 5,700 students studied there, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a> had been annexed to the Duchy of Berg in 1806, and on 17 December 1811 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> decreed the founding of a state university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> and the closure of the academy in its favour. The order was not reversed after his fall, and even the creation of the Duchy of Nassau did not restore the academy.</p><p>Only the theological faculty survived, as the seminary that became the Theological Seminary of the Evangelical Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and Nassau, now in Herborn Castle. Among its former students were the Hebraist Johannes Buxtorf, the polymath Samuel Hartlib, Ludwig von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, inventor of the mezzotint, Philip William Otterbein, founder of the United Brethren in Christ, and the educational reformer Adolph Diesterweg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herborn Academy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John VI adopted Reformed Protestantism and founded the <b>Herborn Academy</b> in 1584.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">County of Nassau-Dillenburg</a></li></ul></section>
